intro.bodyMarkdown:
An object can hold functions as well as data. A function stored in an object is called a **method**, and you have used plenty: `list.push()`, `text.toUpperCase()`. You can write your own, with a short form that drops the `function` word:

```
const dog = {
  name: "Rex",
  speak() {
    return "Woof!"
  },
}
console.log(dog.speak())
```

A method usually needs the object's own data. Inside a method, **`this`** means "the object this method was called on":

```
const dog = {
  name: "Rex",
  introduce() {
    return `I am ${this.name}`
  },
}
console.log(dog.introduce())
```

Because `this` is worked out when the method is called, the same method can serve many objects. `this` is whatever comes before the dot in the call:

```
function introduce() {
  return `I am ${this.name}`
}
const rex = { name: "Rex", introduce }
const tom = { name: "Tom", introduce }
console.log(rex.introduce(), tom.introduce())
```

Methods can change the object too, which makes an object a little machine with its own state:

```
const counter = {
  count: 0,
  add() {
    this.count++
  },
}
counter.add()
counter.add()
console.log(counter.count)
```

Don't use arrow functions for methods: arrows don't get their own `this`, so `this.name` wouldn't mean the object. Use the short method form, `name() { … }`.

