intro.bodyMarkdown:
Most methods work on one instance. Some things belong to the **class as a whole**: a count of how many have been made, a setting all instances share, or a helper for making instances. Mark those **`static`**.

```
class Temperature {
  static freezing = 0

  constructor(celsius) {
    this.celsius = celsius
  }

  static fromFahrenheit(f) {
    return new Temperature((f - 32) / 1.8)
  }
}

const t = Temperature.fromFahrenheit(212)
console.log(t.celsius, Temperature.freezing)
```

Static members are used through the **class name**, `Temperature.fromFahrenheit(…)`, not through an instance. `t.fromFahrenheit` is `undefined`.

You have used many: `Math.max`, `Number.isInteger`, `Array.isArray`, `Object.keys` are all static methods of built-in classes.

A common use is a **factory method**: a named way to build an instance from some other form of data, like `Temperature.fromFahrenheit` or `User.fromJSON(text)`. Another is a class-wide counter:

```
class Ticket {
  static made = 0

  constructor(event) {
    Ticket.made++
    this.event = event
    this.number = Ticket.made
  }
}

new Ticket("gig")
const t = new Ticket("gig")
console.log(t.number, Ticket.made)
```

Fields can also be set right in the class body without `static`, like `items = []`. Each instance then gets its own, as if the constructor had set it. It is a tidy way to give starting values.

```
class Cart {
  items = []
  add(item) {
    this.items.push(item)
  }
}
const c = new Cart()
c.add("tea")
console.log(c.items)
```

