intro.bodyMarkdown:
Often one kind of thing is a special case of another: a savings account *is an* account, with interest. Instead of copying the whole `Account` class, a new class can **extend** it and get everything it has for free:

```
class Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name
  }
  describe() {
    return `${this.name} is an animal`
  }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  bark() {
    return `${this.name} says Woof`
  }
}

const rex = new Dog("Rex")
console.log(rex.describe())
console.log(rex.bark())
```

`Dog` is the **subclass** (or child) and `Animal` the **superclass** (parent). A `Dog` has everything an `Animal` has, plus its own extras. And `rex instanceof Dog` and `rex instanceof Animal` are both `true`.

When the subclass needs its own constructor, it must call **`super(…)`** first, which runs the parent's constructor to set up the shared part. Only after that can it use `this`:

```
class Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name
  }
}

class Cat extends Animal {
  constructor(name, indoor) {
    super(name)
    this.indoor = indoor
  }
}

console.log(new Cat("Tom", true))
```

Forget `super(name)` and you get a `ReferenceError` as soon as the constructor touches `this`.

Use inheritance for a real "is a" relationship, and keep the chain short. A subclass that would need to undo half of what its parent does is a sign the parent is the wrong shape.

