intro.bodyMarkdown:
Programs save things: to a file, to the browser's storage, or over the network. All of those take plain **data**, usually JSON text, not living objects with methods. So a class needs a way to turn an instance into data, and a way to rebuild an instance from it.

`JSON.stringify` turns an object into text, keeping its own public properties. Methods, getters and private fields are left out:

```
class Task {
  #secret = "hidden"
  constructor(title) {
    this.title = title
    this.done = false
  }
  toggle() {
    this.done = !this.done
  }
}

const t = new Task("Wash up")
const text = JSON.stringify(t)
console.log(text)
```

Going back, `JSON.parse(text)` gives a **plain object**, not a `Task`: it has the data but no `toggle` method, and `instanceof Task` is `false`. The usual fix is a static **`fromJSON`** factory that builds a real instance from the data:

```
class Task {
  constructor(title, done = false) {
    this.title = title
    this.done = done
  }
  toggle() {
    this.done = !this.done
  }
  static fromJSON(data) {
    return new Task(data.title, data.done)
  }
}

const data = JSON.parse('{"title":"Wash up","done":true}')
const t = Task.fromJSON(data)
t.toggle()
console.log(t instanceof Task, t.done)
```

To control what gets saved, give the class a **`toJSON()`** method: `JSON.stringify` calls it and saves whatever it returns. That is how you include private data, leave out things that shouldn't be saved, or add a version number.

```
class Account {
  #balance
  constructor(owner, balance = 0) {
    this.owner = owner
    this.#balance = balance
  }
  toJSON() {
    return { owner: this.owner, balance: this.#balance }
  }
}
console.log(JSON.stringify(new Account("Kim", 50)))
```

The same goes for copies: `{ ...task }` copies the data into a plain object, losing the class. To copy an instance, make a new one: `Task.fromJSON(task.toJSON?.() ?? task)`, or give the class its own `clone()` method.

